WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.715
Hi, I'm Mortise!

00:00:02.715 --> 00:00:06.399
Today: how a splatting model cleans a noisy point cloud without

00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:07.739
moving a single point.

00:00:09.558 --> 00:00:10.830
Leave the geometry alone.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.693
Every point stays where the depth model put it.

00:00:13.693 --> 00:00:16.555
The network only learns how visible each one is.

00:00:19.092 --> 00:00:22.426
The images go into a frozen copy of Depth Anything 3.

00:00:22.426 --> 00:00:25.154
It predicts depth and a camera for every view.

00:00:25.574 --> 00:00:28.230
Back-project every pixel to get a point cloud.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.554
Good on average, noisy at every edge.

00:00:30.974 --> 00:00:35.100
A second copy, with only its last ten blocks trained, feeds a

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.195
small head: rotation, scale, opacity and colour, per pixel.

00:00:38.615 --> 00:00:40.439
Centres come only from the depth.

00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:42.262
The head never outputs a position.

00:00:43.608 --> 00:00:44.849
Then three cheap steps.

00:00:46.197 --> 00:00:50.251
Divide by the average point distance, so a street and a table look alike.

00:00:50.671 --> 00:00:53.209
Snap Gaussians to a grid and average each cell.

00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:55.007
Delete anything nearly transparent.

00:00:55.007 --> 00:00:57.074
That is where the noise goes.

00:00:58.942 --> 00:01:02.663
The tempting fix is letting the network nudge points onto surfaces.

00:01:02.663 --> 00:01:05.030
In the paper's tests, renders got worse.

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:09.666
Frozen positions leave the loss one way out: shape, colour and opacity.

00:01:11.758 --> 00:01:15.953
Labelled with true depth, inconsistent points pile up near zero opacity.

00:01:15.953 --> 00:01:17.860
Everything else sits near one.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.814
So the threshold clears the mid-air haze and leaves the shelf alone.

00:01:23.508 --> 00:01:27.542
About half the inconsistent points vanish, and less than a tenth of everything.

00:01:30.842 --> 00:01:32.977
On perceptual error it leads clearly.

00:01:32.977 --> 00:01:36.534
On plain pixel accuracy, one baseline still edges it out.

00:01:38.892 --> 00:01:41.832
Opacity can delete what floats in front of a surface.

00:01:41.832 --> 00:01:44.772
It cannot move a surface the depth model got wrong.

00:01:47.608 --> 00:01:52.323
Frozen depth for position, learned attributes for the rest, and a threshold that cleans.

00:01:52.743 --> 00:01:54.852
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:54.852 --> 00:01:55.455
I'm Mortise.

00:01:55.455 --> 00:01:55.756
Bye!
